{% from "agent-prompt.njk" import agentPrompt %}

<div class="content">
  <h1>Switching from point API to direct access</h1>
  <p>
    The <a href="/api/">JSON point API</a> provides convenient access when running Python is
    difficult but a curl request or web fetch is easy. For most work, direct access to the
    <a href="/catalog/">catalog's Icechunk Zarr stores</a> is the better option: it is more
    flexible, can be much faster for larger reads, and is free to access without the point
    API's request or query-size limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    If that sounds intimidating, don't worry! You can open a dataset and read the values you
    need with just a few lines of Python. If you use a coding agent, give it this prompt:
  </p>
  {% set directAccessPrompt %}Help me switch from the Dynamical point API to direct dataset access. Read https://dynamical.org/api/direct-access/ and https://dynamical.org/llms.txt, then follow https://dynamical.org/prompt.md to set up Python and dynamical-catalog in this project. Use my existing API request to identify the dataset, variables, location, run and time range; ask me for those details if they are missing. Resolve the dataset from the current Dynamical catalog, check its dimensions, units and terms, and select only the data I need before loading it. Run one small read, show the selected coordinates and values, and give me the working code. Preserve missing values rather than treating them as a complete forecast.{% endset %}
  {{ agentPrompt(directAccessPrompt, "Direct-access prompt for a coding agent", "api-direct-access", 8) }}

  <h2>Opening a data product directly</h2>
  <p>
    Opening a data product takes just an import and one function call. Choose a dataset from the
    <a href="/catalog/">catalog</a> and copy its ID. For example, to open the
    <a href="/catalog/noaa-gfs-forecast/">GFS forecast</a>:
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "py" %}import dynamical_catalog

ds = dynamical_catalog.open("noaa-gfs-forecast", chunks=None){% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    You need Python 3.12 or newer, <code>dynamical-catalog</code>, Icechunk 2 and xarray. Save the
    code as <code>direct_access.py</code>; <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/">uv</a> can install
    those packages and run it in one command:
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "bash" %}uv run --with "dynamical-catalog>=1.1.0" --with "icechunk>=2,<3" --with xarray direct_access.py{% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    Opening the dataset reads its metadata, not the whole archive. The package looks up the
    current storage location in the <a href="https://stac.dynamical.org/catalog.json">STAC
    catalog</a>, so you don't need to track buckets or versioned URLs. Catalog pages provide a
    minimal opening snippet and example notebooks, along with variables, dimensions, units and
    coverage. Use the examples for your chosen dataset.
  </p>

  <h2>Read a forecast at one point</h2>
  <p>
    Add this below the opening code to read temperature at the nearest grid point to Chicago,
    for the first three lead times of the latest GFS forecast:
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "py" %}point = (
    ds["temperature_2m"]
    .isel(init_time=-1, lead_time=slice(0, 3))
    .sel(latitude=41.88, longitude=-87.63, method="nearest")
)
point.load()
print(point)
print(point.attrs.get("units")){% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    <code>init_time</code> chooses the forecast run; <code>lead_time</code> is time after that run.
    For reproducible work, choose an exact initialization time from <code>ds.init_time</code>
    instead of the last run. Nearest means nearest grid cell, not interpolation. Other datasets
    can have different time or spatial coordinates; their catalog examples show how to select them.
  </p>

  <h2>Read only what you need</h2>
  <p>
    Select variables, times and locations before calling <code>load()</code> or exporting.
    Large selections can use a lot of memory, so process them in pieces. Read the dataset page's
    License and Attribution sections for its terms and any geographic restrictions. Direct access
    avoids the point API's limits; data-source rate limits and terms still apply.
  </p>
</div>
